WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.227 --> 00:00:04.918
- The diversity and the
range of Flemish patterns,

00:00:04.918 --> 00:00:07.183
and colors, and forms of the chickens

00:00:07.183 --> 00:00:09.257
are just fascinating.

00:00:09.257 --> 00:00:10.857
You've really got to like it.

00:00:10.857 --> 00:00:12.543
It's kind of dusty, dirty, noisy.

00:00:12.543 --> 00:00:13.743
(chickens crowing)

00:00:13.743 --> 00:00:14.752
Very noisy.

00:00:14.752 --> 00:00:18.329
But, it's sort of fun and
gratifying in its way.

00:00:18.329 --> 00:00:21.412
(chickens squawking)

00:00:23.752 --> 00:00:27.364
(fast-paced cello music)

00:00:27.364 --> 00:00:29.675
I'm Tom Whiting, owner
and founder of Whiting Farms

00:00:29.675 --> 00:00:31.481
in Delta, Colorado.

00:00:31.481 --> 00:00:33.735
We're a poultry genetics
and production company

00:00:33.735 --> 00:00:36.452
that specializes in fly tying feathers.

00:00:36.452 --> 00:00:38.119
For reasons I can't even remember why

00:00:38.119 --> 00:00:41.234
I started raising poultry when
I was about 10 years of age

00:00:41.234 --> 00:00:45.401
and I just never cease to find
it an interesting subject.

00:00:47.365 --> 00:00:50.115
(plastic scraping)

00:00:50.115 --> 00:00:52.338
I'm sure most people think
they know what feathers are.

00:00:52.338 --> 00:00:54.973
But really feathers are highly specialized

00:00:54.973 --> 00:00:57.140
elongated reptilian scale.

00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:01.119
It's just evolved into feathers.

00:01:01.119 --> 00:01:03.254
A major function is sexual selection

00:01:03.254 --> 00:01:05.978
meaning that the roosters
have very brilliant

00:01:05.978 --> 00:01:07.238
and very showy feathers.

00:01:07.238 --> 00:01:09.506
So, the rooster with the finest feathers

00:01:09.506 --> 00:01:11.512
might get the most hens.

00:01:11.512 --> 00:01:15.348
And those feather traits
get increased over time.

00:01:15.348 --> 00:01:17.506
It takes almost a year
to grow the roosters

00:01:17.506 --> 00:01:19.035
from baby chicks.

00:01:19.035 --> 00:01:23.349
I think we've settled in the
80,000 range at any one time.

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:25.055
Chickens, because they're small

00:01:25.055 --> 00:01:27.022
and they reproduce very quickly,

00:01:27.022 --> 00:01:30.661
we can radically alter how
the birds grow their feathers.

00:01:30.661 --> 00:01:32.100
(chickens squawking)

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:36.503
It's amazing how much with
selection the birds can have

00:01:36.503 --> 00:01:40.586
their feathers change over
times and generations.

00:01:42.994 --> 00:01:44.841
This one's going to Colorado,

00:01:44.841 --> 00:01:45.973
Salt Lake City,

00:01:45.973 --> 00:01:46.990
Massachusetts,

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:48.578
Sweden, Germany, Norway,

00:01:48.578 --> 00:01:50.687
Wales, Denmark, Kenya.

00:01:50.687 --> 00:01:52.268
This whole order right
here I know is going

00:01:52.268 --> 00:01:54.702
waiting to go to Japan.

00:01:54.702 --> 00:01:57.336
Rooster feathers are used
in tying fishing flies

00:01:57.336 --> 00:01:59.547
or they have been for
hundreds of years we know.

00:01:59.547 --> 00:02:01.654
Because they're a defensive feather

00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:03.733
so they have some rigidity to them

00:02:03.733 --> 00:02:06.017
that rests on the surface
tension of the water

00:02:06.017 --> 00:02:07.893
and it looks like an insect to the fish.

00:02:07.893 --> 00:02:09.244
And the fish will see it and the dimples

00:02:09.244 --> 00:02:10.895
it makes in the water,

00:02:10.895 --> 00:02:12.520
and they'll want to strike that.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.620
And that's the way the
fisherman catches the fish.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.250
(line whooshing)

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:20.272
One of the reasons I'm in
this business is I like

00:02:20.272 --> 00:02:22.629
just dealing with the birds.

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:24.517
I find them endlessly fascinating.

00:02:24.517 --> 00:02:27.009
And my favorite time of day
is often when I'm going out

00:02:27.009 --> 00:02:29.329
through the birds on a daily basis

00:02:29.329 --> 00:02:31.398
and looking at them,
seeing how they're doing.

00:02:31.398 --> 00:02:32.944
And I feel like I know them intimately

00:02:32.944 --> 00:02:36.111
and I handle every bird in the system.

00:02:37.055 --> 00:02:38.957
I've seen the trajectory or the arc

00:02:38.957 --> 00:02:40.856
of where the feathers have come from.

00:02:40.856 --> 00:02:43.628
But I don't view this as
something that I'm doing,

00:02:43.628 --> 00:02:46.079
it's rather I'm doing it for a while.

00:02:46.079 --> 00:02:49.431
And I feel a responsibility
to cultivate them

00:02:49.431 --> 00:02:50.880
in a responsible way,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.435
protect them from diseases
so they don't get wiped out,

00:02:54.435 --> 00:02:56.707
and so they'll be available
for as long as people

00:02:56.707 --> 00:02:58.874
want to tie fishing flies.

